re detail so be sure to check that one  out  um  but all of these enzymes particularly  the flip basis and block bases you know  they catalyze this diffusion really to  maintain  membrane  asymmetry  and what that means is that with these  biological membranes  you know all of these phospholipids  they're not all the same there's a lot  of different kinds of phospholipids um  phosphatidylserine phosphatidylcholine  phosphatidyl ethanol ethanol amine um  single myelin uh cholesterol right well  cholesterol is a steroid on a  phospholipid but the point is that  there's a lot of different uh  lipids  in this bilayer some of them are much  more frequent in the exoplasmic face  some are much more frequent in the  cytoplasmic face and the having the  right frequencies the right  phospholipids on the right sides of that  membrane is really important for  membrane function so that's what we call  membrane asymmetry having like different  phospholipids in different places  basically on in one leaflet versus  another leaflet and so these enzymes  help to maintain that now these enzymes  are trans membrane right the flip faces  the flop bases the scram blazes they  span the membrane that makes them trans  membrane proteins and their lipid  transporters so they're transporting  phospholipids from one side to another  now these may be highly specific  meaning that they may only transport  like one kind of  uh one kind of phospholipid or they may  transport multiple types it just kind of  depends on which protein you're talking  about  but really importantly they provide a  way for hydrophilic heads to move across  the hydrophobic bilayer middle and i've  kind of got that  illustrated up here so here we've got a  membrane this right here what i've drawn  is a scramblace  scramblaces are calcium dependent as  we'll see in a minute but in order for  uh  this scramblace in in this instance to  move phospholipids from one side of the  membrane to another side meaning from  like the exoplasmic side on the outside  to the cytoplasmic side on the inside or  vice versa they they do so  by providing a mechanism through which  this you know hydrophilic so these all  of these head groups you see the little  green dash that's a minus sign a  negative sign  and so these  heads both the ones that are down here  and the ones that are up here  they are very polar  very hydrophilic so i'm going to write  that right here  these heads are hydrophilic remember  that's water loving  and very polar  so they like to  interact with water and other polar  things  on the other hand those fatty acid tails  that are in the middle of the bilayer  that are part of those phospholipids  those are very water fearing remember  that means that they are hydrophobic  and non-polar  and these things yeah they're like water  and oil right they don't like to mix so  thermodynamically that is not favored  so  really you know all of these types but  here we're talking about scrub laces and  in particular the scramblaces  offer a way for these negatively charged  uh  negatively charged phospholipid heads  to be able to cross to flip-flop to  transversely diffuse from one side to  the other side  um through that very hydrophobic  nonpolar barrier  so that's kind of what the mechanism  looks like  now let's keep talking about scramblaces  in humans there are five different  scramblaces we call them  hplscr1  through i should have a little h right  here too through  hplscr5 so five different ones they have  differential expression between tissues  some of them are expressed in lots of  different tissues  whereas for example hplcr2  has only been found to be expressed in  the male testis  so  differential expressions between tissues  and then scrambling  they're distinct from flip faces and  flaw bases so again if you're interested  in flip faces and flaw pieces see my  other video on that but the scram laces  they've got some different  characteristics  first of all they're energy independent  they do not  require atp  so flip aces and flop bases they have  atp hydrolysis that's like an energy  investment from the cell  to  drive forward their action for them to  do their job  on the other hand scramblace is energy  independent no atp hydrolysis required  on the other hand scramblacies are  calcium dependent so the scramblace  complexes  they have  a calcium ion binding domain for those  of you interested in protein domains  it's like an ef hand like domain but  anyways it binds calcium  now normally at like a resting sort of  normal state of the cell  normally  there's low calcium in the cells and  this means since calcium i'll circle  that right there since calcium binding  is necessary for the scramblace to  function if there's low calcium in the  cells there tends to be  low scramblace activity now remember  that the  i talked earlier about this membrane  asymmetry and how important it is that  certain  phospholipids be  very frequent in the exoplasmic face and  other phospholipids be very frequent in  the cytoplasmic face  well scramblace i mean the way that it  works is by  scrambling  these phospholipids moving them in in  either direction so that's called  bi-directional moving from  outside to the inside or from the inside  face to the outside face  and so  one of the  ways this happens or is triggered is  through increased cytosolic calcium so  when you get  an increase i'm going to have some up  arrows right there when you get some  increase in calcium ions in the  cytosolic the inside  compartment of the cell you also get  increased  scramblace activity  and so what it does is it scrambles  these negatively  charged phospholipids  bi-directionally  remember flip faces and flop bases uh  one direction only flip base is going  from  exoplasmic phase to cytoplasmic face  flaw bases moving things in the opposite  direction  scram blazes either direction  bi-directional  so scramble these negatively charged  phospholipids  giving them a pathway to be able to  cross through that hydrophobic fatty  acid  tail portion of the bilayer  and and then be  basically flipped to the other side  so this scrambles mediated transport is  particularly  important there's a few different  um  phenomena i guess you could say cellular  phenomena where we where we see this a  lot and one  is with the  um  with the phospholipid phosphatidylserine  so  phosphatidylserine  gets transported  from the inside cytosolic or cytoplasmic  leaflet to the exoplasmic leaflet now  it's important that you realize that  about 96  of phosphatidylserine  is usually in the inside leaflet okay so  like in the cytoplasmic face  um so usually 96 of this on the inside  but  scrambling mediated transport scrambles  moving phosphatidylserine  from the inner leaflet to the outer  leaflet  is an early step in a process known as  apoptosis  and maybe you've heard of this apoptosis  is programmed cell  death it is the  regulated organized  breakdown and death of a cell and it's a  normal part  of functioning in multicellular  organisms  and so  having that phosphatidylserine  suddenly appearing at much higher  frequencies on the outside of the cell  that actually is an early step in  apoptosis and it signals  to  uh basically passing immune cells called  macrophages  to engulf  and and kill the cell and this is a way  that  um that like old or damaged cells can be  broken down and their parts recycled  basically  so that is scramblacies  i now want to take a minute to talk  about a different  class  of these  transmembrane lipid transporter proteins  and those are known as  biogenic  flip aces and i talk a little bit more  about biogenic flip bases in my video on  flip bases and flop bases and the reason  i bring them up here  is because even though they are  often called flip aces  they are sometimes classified as  scramblaces in other words depending on  where you're reading about them what  source you're using sometimes they'll be  referred to as flip bases and sometimes  they'll be called scramblaces  now  why is this why are they sometimes  called scramblaces well they have some  things in common with scramblaces you  know they are atp independent remember  that flip faces hydrolyze atp they  require atp to do their job  whereas these  lipid transporter proteins are atp  independent no atp required like  scramblacies they're also bi-directional  remember  scramblaces  i think you can see it best here  scramblaces are moving phospholipids in  either direction in both directions  whereas flip bases only moving from the  exoplasmic to the cytoplasmic side of  the membrane  so in this way the biogenic flip bases  are very similar to scramblacies  but then in other ways they're not very  similar to scrambling scramblacies they  are constitutively active so they're not  activated through this like calcium  binding domain  they also even though they're  bi-directional moving things in either  direction across the membrane  they are  really  especially important for movement in us  in a particular direction  and that is because phospholipids that  are made in the endoplasmic reticulum in  the lumen of the endoplasmic reticulum  um or in the bacterial plasma membrane  they have to be  moved they have to be moved from the  basically from the er lumen to the  cytoplasmic side  um and that is very similar to what you  know  basically regular flip bases do moving  from  from from one from from the original  destination from the original location  sorry from the original location to the  cytoplasmic side  biogenic bases are moving from the er  lumen to  the cytoplasmic side i should probably  write that right here er lumen  2  the  cytoplasmic or cytosolic  side  okay  or if they're in bacterial plasma  membranes to the outer leaflet  um and i guess i want to say one more  thing this word right here  biogenic  this is referring to  membranes  where  where where membrane is being created or  synthesized so particularly in the  endoplasmic reticulum also the golgi  apparatus and eukaryotic cells  this is where our membranes are being  made being synthesized so that's why  it's called biogenic  so we are talking about the lipid  transporters that are  playing a role in those biogenic  membranes in the er  so  if you want to learn more i know i've  already mentioned that video on flip  faces and flaw bases  i also have a video on membrane  asymmetry and sightedness if you want to  learn more about  you know which phospholipids are present  on either side and how that happens and  why it's important  and i guess otherwise that's it for  today so thank you so much for watching  biology professor please please please  make sure that you subscribe hit the  subscribe button hit the bell to get  notifications and good luck studying i  will see you next time thanks guys

